Ports upstream PR vllm-project/vllm#61050 (open) to this pin.

Keeps a running request's first chunks in the CPU KV tier until the request finishes. Since vllm #51787 the CPU
offloading manager applies a request's cache recency once, in on_request_finished, ranking its chunks head to tail.
Until then each chunk keeps the rank it got when it was stored, head oldest, so when the tier fills while a request
is still running (one long conversation's prefill while another decodes), the evictions take chunk 0 of every
KV-cache group from the request that has not finished, and its next turn's lookup, which starts at chunk 0, finds
nothing.

The PR gives each running request one provisional LRU rank when the manager first sees it and ranks its positioned
keys by (request rank, -token position), so the request's tail goes before its head across store batches without
revisiting the prefix. on_request_finished still commits the recency; ARC and other policies get a no-op hook.

Measured on a native RTX 3090 with alternative.sh, an 8 GiB tier and two ~60K conversations in flight, one boot
each: on turn 2, 0.31.0 reuses 0 and 0 tokens, this reuses 50,880 and 49,184 (85.2% and 82.1%) with 1.90 GB read
back and no chunk 0 evicted, and 0.30.0 reuses 96.3% for both. A re-rank of the request's whole prefix after each
store batch gave the same counts to the token.

Applied unchanged: the PR cherry-picks onto 0.31.0 without conflicts, its tests included.

Kind: fix
What: the CPU KV tier keeps a running request's head chunks; an eviction before the request finishes takes its tail
  first
Upstream: vllm #61050 (open); the regression is from vllm #51787
Cut-against: 0.32.0
Retires-when: the pin that carries #61050

